SUMMARY:Horny Gods\, Lying Heroes: Four Weeks Inside The Odyssey
DESCRIPTION:In this four-part Olio\, Dina Odnopozova (PhD Yale\, professor 
 at Baruch)&nbsp\; will approach the Odyssey as a vast encyclopedia of anci
 ent knowledge. We will delve in its social mores\, traditions\, debates\, 
 prejudices\, laws\, etymologies\, meals\, rituals\, maps and horny gods. W
 hile the epic describes a distant culture\, it also contains a repository 
 of psychological archetypes that we encounter in our nightmares and indivi
 dual journeys: apples of discord that prey on our vanity\, cyclopes who th
 reaten us with sheer otherness\, green herbs and beautiful strangers that 
 make us forget the way home. Even before the first trailers dropped\, the 
 movie became an apple of discord that stirred polarizing debates about rac
 e\, identity\, gender\, otherness\, appropriation\, ownerships and possess
 ion. This media Trojan war touches upon the very questions raised by the o
 riginal epic: What does it mean to be away from home? What is home? What m
 akes one an outsider? How does one treat a stranger according to the laws 
 of hospitality? How does one treat a stranger if he is a “barbarian”? 
 Or a monster? What is a monster? And what's up with all the terrifying fem
 ale monsters that haunt Odysseus?&nbsp\;Homer frequently called Odysseus p
 olymetis—a word that has troubled translators for centuries. It has been
  rendered as "ingenious\," "multitalented\," "crafty-minded\," and "skille
 d in all ways of contriving.” Looking past the heroic status Odysseus ha
 s accumulated over millennia\, the most accurate modern translation of thi
 s epithet might just be a "lying asshole." While his faithful wife Penelop
 e weaves and unweaves a shroud to delay remarriage\, Odysseus weaves endle
 ss stories out of lies and tricks. He spends his journey cheating on his w
 ife\, breaking and entering\, stealing cheese from mythical beasts\, gasli
 ghting his crew until they all die\, and acting like a privileged brat all
  along. So\, what makes him one of the most popular heroes of all time? Wh
 y does his story still capture our cultural imagination\, as evidenced by 
 the massive craze surrounding Christopher Nolan’s adaptation?SessionsFou
 r sessions\, Tuesdays 7pm-9pm\, starting October 6\, 13\, 20\, 27 at the O
 lio Lighthouse.Each class focuses on a single section of The Odyssey. You 
 are encouraged to read each section prior to class.Telemachy (Books I–IV
 )\, Wanderings (Books V–XII)\, Homecoming (Books XIII–XIX) and Vengean
 ce (Books XX–XXIV).\n\nTickets & info: https://www.viewcy.com/event/odys
 sey
URL:https://www.viewcy.com/event/odyssey
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
